# Tenant-kind twin of ./../service/schedule_backup.rhai (../service/schedule_backup.sdd):
# an actual `diff` shows one hunk, the per-document apply call on line 22. Twin claims below
# were re-verified against both sources, not copied.

Spec: TenantScheduleBackupScript

Platform: Rhai script library (vynil-core 0.7.7 engine)

Purpose:
  Keep the periodic restic backup schedule of one `TenantInstance` applied: invoked from the
    tenant install lane's backup branch, render the packaged `cronbackup.yaml` partial into the
    instance's schedule objects (scripts ConfigMap, ServiceAccount, RoleBinding and a CronJob
    whose Pod dispatches the package type's own `backup` lane) and apply them through the
    tenant-single-object installer, so the tenant's namespaces act as the apply-namespace
    router and backups ride the tenant maintenance-window cron (minted hour `1`). All
    env/volume/schedule inputs come from @bctx::run(@instance, @context, `false`) — this file
    computes no cron expression, reads no retention value and runs no restic itself. It is the
    CR-side stage owning @set_status_schedule_backup_failed on @TenantInstance and re-throws
    into the install lane's exit-1.

Exposes:
  @run(@instance, @context) — the module's sole @fn and entry point (`schedule_backup.rhai` +
    `run` match the entry-point patterns of /agent/src/linting/rhai_checker.sdd); reached ONLY
    through @import_run("schedule_backup", instance, context) in ./install.rhai's backup branch
    — no Rust lane names the module, and the caller discards the return value whole
    (./install.sdd). A package `scripts/schedule_backup.rhai` shadowing the name replaces this
    whole lane on that install.

Accepts:
  @instance — the injected @TenantInstance @Dynamic (fetched by @TenantInstance::get in the
    Rust lane, /agent/src/tenant/install.sdd), as last re-bound by ./install.rhai (a refreshed
    @get_tenant_instance copy whenever an earlier bucket lane was taken — ./install.sdd).
    Direct reads in this file: `.metadata.namespace` (the fallback apply-namespace argument)
    and the @set_status_schedule_backup_failed method on failure. Everything else flows
    through collaborators on the same object: @bctx::run requires `.status.vitals` (array,
    unguarded) and `.status.scalables` (guarded); `spec.initFrom` is never read at this call
    site (third arg `false`).
  @context — the install-lane map (the same object ./install.rhai threads to every bucket;
    producer ./context.rhai). Direct reads in this file: `template_dir` (unguarded
    property-on-`()` if non-map; args default `./agent/templates` per
    /agent/src/tenant/install.sdd), the `${context.package_dir}/pods` gate, and
    `tenant.namespaces` — the @install_allowed_obj allow-list argument, read per document and
    unguarded: `tenant` is minted by ./context.rhai but a `*_pre`/`*_prepare` whole-map hook
    replacement can erase it, see Raises. The whole map is also @bctx::run's data source and
    the Handlebars render data, so the shipped templates read `instance.appslug`/`.namespace`,
    `cluster.vynil_namespace`, `agent_image`, `schedule` and the
    `envs`/`envs_from`/`volumes`/`mounts`/`files`/`has_files`/`backup_affinity` set — that key
    contract is owned by ./context.sdd + ./context_tenant.sdd + ./../lib/build_context.sdd +
    ./../lib/backup_context.sdd, not here (the `namespace` map @bctx::run reads is the same
    map aliased onto `tenant` by ./context_tenant.rhai). The map's `instance` key (built view)
    is NOT the injected CR.
  Hooks — three optional @import_run module names: `schedule_backup_pre`,
    `schedule_backup_prepare`, `schedule_backup_post`, each called with the same
    (instance, context) pair. None ships in either tenant resolver script directory (./,
    ./../lib) — package `scripts/` slots only.

Returns:
  The `context` map as held after the try/catch, plus a `schedule_backup_post` merge on a map
    return — a value ./install.rhai discards whole (./install.sdd); the lane communicates only
    by side effects (each @install_allowed_obj applied-entry list is likewise dropped). The
    observable outcomes: the rendered documents applied in decoded-document order — shipped
    partials yield `{appslug}-backups-scripts` ConfigMap (only when `has_files`),
    ServiceAccount `{appslug}-backup`, RoleBinding `{appslug}-backup` (roleRef ClusterRole
    `${cluster.vynil_namespace}:backups`, subject SA in `instance.namespace`) and CronJob
    `{appslug}-backups` with `spec.schedule` = the `schedule` context text. No shipped document
    carries its own `metadata.namespace`, so all four resolve to and apply in
    `instance.metadata.namespace` (@install_allowed_obj rewrites `metadata.namespace` into
    every namespaced document); a document carrying its own namespace applies THERE when it is
    in `tenant.namespaces`, else silently into the instance namespace, and a cluster-scoped
    document is only warn-ignored (unlike the service twin). Job-template labels are
    `instance.selector` plus `app.kubernetes.io/component=backup` emitted by
    @selector_from_ctx (/common/src/handlebarshandler.sdd) — the component label is what
    ./delete_backup.rhai's Job sweep later filters on; and the `ScheduleBackup` failure
    condition on error.

Raises:
  Zero error-fabricating @throw statement: the file's one throw is the bare `throw;` re-throw
    after the failure marker. @import_run swallows only the absent-module and absent-function
    skip markers (`No ${name} module, skipping.` / `No ${name}::run function, skipping.`) —
    everything else propagates.
  Inside the @try (marker path): a raw raise from @bctx::run (dead/absent `status.vitals`,
    missing `instance` context key, the Cluster-branch Secrets @list_meta read — its spec's
    Raises), from either @register_partial_dir call on a broken/unreadable partial directory,
    from @render_from or @yaml_decode_multi, from the `context.tenant.namespaces` argument
    read once the `tenant` key is gone (property-on-`()`), the engine error of a non-map
    document reaching @install_allowed_obj's `.metadata.namespace` dot (no
    @get_objects-style qualification filter runs on this stream), @install_allowed_obj's
    `kind: ${obj.kind} is unknown from the apiServer.` throw, or an apply KubeError — becomes
    @set_status_schedule_backup_failed(reason) (verbatim string; non-string through
    @json_encode) then `throw;`: the install lane reaches no later bucket and exits 1
    (/agent/src/tenant/install.sdd), condition `ScheduleBackup`/`Warning`
    `ScheduleBackupFailed` with truncated note (/common/src/instance_macros.sdd,
    /common/src/instancetenant.sdd).
  A failure on the N-th document leaves documents 1..N-1 applied and the rest never applied —
    no rollback, the marker alone records it.
  Outside the @try: @set_status_schedule_backup_failed's own KubeError replaces the original
    error; a raise inside the `schedule_backup_pre` hook kills the lane with no marker at all;
    a raise inside the `schedule_backup_post` hook (the only statement after the try) likewise
    leaves no marker even though the full schedule already applied (Tasks).
  @json_encode of an unserializable caught payload in the `_` switch arm can itself raise out
    of the catch, replacing the reason (same open point as ./../lib/backup_context.sdd).

Handles:
  Stock image: all three hook names take the debug-log @import_run skip, the @type_of guard
    sees no map, and the lane runs on the unchanged `context`. A hook returning a non-map
    leaves the previous `context` in force (same merge discipline as ./install.sdd's buckets).
  Partial precedence: `template_dir` registers first, then `${package_dir}/pods` (only under
    @is_dir) registers second, so a package `pods/cronbackup.yaml.hbs` or `pods/backup.yaml.hbs`
    silently overwrites the shipped same-name partial (partial name = filename minus `.hbs`).
  The optional scripts ConfigMap is gated INSIDE the template (`{{#if has_files}}`), not by
    this file: the loop blindly applies whatever documents the stream holds.
  Allow-list degeneracy: `tenant.namespaces` is passed as the raw context value — per
    ./context.sdd Raises the upstream getter can land it as the plain string `""`; what the
    allow check then does with a non-array is unpinned (irrelevant to the shipped,
    namespace-less documents — Tasks).
  No dedup probe, no wait, no inventory — unlike ./../service/init_from.sdd (Job-exist gate +
    @wait::all; this directory's ./init_from.rhai carries the same posture), every document is
    unconditionally re-applied on every qualifying install and every @install_allowed_obj
    return is dropped; nothing is recorded into `status`.
  Failure text shape: the marker reason is the caught engine error's own text — verbatim for
    thrown strings, @json_encode text otherwise — no wrapper or file/line prefix at this level.

Must:
  Execute exactly: @import_run("schedule_backup_pre") + map-merge guard → @try { @bctx::run
    (@instance, @context, `false`) → @import_run("schedule_backup_prepare") + map-merge →
    @new_hbs → @register_partial_dir(`context.template_dir`) → @is_dir-gated
    @register_partial_dir(`${context.package_dir}/pods`) → @yaml_decode_multi of @render_from
    (`"{{> cronbackup.yaml }}"`, @context) → @dir::install_allowed_obj(`obj`,
    `context.tenant.namespaces`, `instance.metadata.namespace`) per document in stream order }
    → on raise @set_status_schedule_backup_failed + `throw;` →
    @import_run("schedule_backup_post") + map-merge → return `context`; nothing else, in no
    other order. This is the twin's whole chain with only the apply call swapped.
  Call @bctx::run with @use_init_from `false` — the schedule-lane posture per
    ./../lib/backup_context.sdd: `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` never emitted, `envs_from` stays the
    `backup-settings` Secret, per-Secret `/secrets/` volumes mounted, and `schedule` set to the
    cron text `${namespace.maintenance_start_minut} ${namespace.maintenance_start_hour} * * *`
    — on this lane the pair comes from the tenant mint (`0`/`1`, ./context.sdd), so the default
    schedule is `0 1 * * *`; that @bctx::run call also takes the tenant `RESTIC_TAGS` branch
    because `["package"].type` is `tenant` (owner ./../lib/backup_context.sdd). NO arithmetic,
    no `RESTIC_MAX_*` retention and no cron-expression building happens in this file or by
    this call site (retention defaults only later, inside the dispatched Pod's ./backup.sdd
    @from_args half).
  Keep the apply posture of ./../lib/install_from_dir.sdd's single-object tenant lane: exactly
    the object-namespace-if-`in`-allow-list-else-instance-namespace resolution (silent
    remap), `metadata.namespace` rewritten on every namespaced apply, cluster-scoped kinds
    warn-ignored and NEVER applied (the twin's `allow_cluster` `true` has no counterpart
    here), one bare apply per document with no readiness wait, no first/middle/last
    bucket-ordering pass — decoded stream order is apply order — and the 0/1-entry applied
    lists dropped.
  Treat idempotence as bare full replay: there is no prior-schedule read, diff or prune; each
    qualifying install re-renders from the fresh `context` (so maintenance-window changes flow
    to the next install's CronJob) and re-applies the same fixed names. Removal lives only in
    ./delete_backup.rhai when ./install.sdd's backup gate flips.
  Write the failure marker with the two switched arms exactly as written (thrown string
    verbatim / other error `json_encode`d) and re-throw; success is never marked — no
    `set_status_*` success call, condition or tag exists for this lane; the install close in
    ./install.sdd stamps `Ready`/`tag` later.
  Emit no @log_info, @log_debug, @log_warn or @log_error line at this level (the file contains
    zero log calls; only imported collaborators log).
  Keep the import-time surface at the two top-level imports — `"backup_context"` as `bctx`,
    `"install_from_dir"` as `dir`, both import-pure per their specs — so module load touches no
    env, filesystem or cluster; a resolver miss on either is an eval error in the install lane
    before @run starts.
  Resolve the module and its three hook names against the four resolver directories frozen by
    the tenant Rust lane (`{package_dir}/scripts`, `{config_dir}`, `{script_dir}/tenant`,
    `{script_dir}/lib` — /agent/src/tenant/install.sdd), so packages can ship the hooks or
    shadow the whole lane.

Must not:
  Open or close a maintenance/scale window: no `maintenance_start`/`maintenance_stop`
    @import_run here (unlike ./backup.sdd and ./restore.sdd) — installing a schedule perturbs
    no workload; only the cron-fired Pod quiesces.
  Read `instance.spec.initFrom` or default any restic env: the `false` third arg is the only
    lever this lane takes on the env contract that ./../lib/backup_context.sdd owns; a second
    reader (or the init lane's `true` posture leaking here) would fork source selection.
  Persist a schedule inventory into `status` (no applied-object list, no success condition):
    the counterpart stage lanes store their buckets — this lane deliberately does not, and
    removal matches by fixed name/labels instead.

Depends on:
  ./../lib/backup_context.rhai — the @run(..., `false`) half of the env/context contract
    (./../lib/backup_context.sdd, which freezes this file as one of its two schedule-lane
    callers).
  ./../lib/install_from_dir.rhai — @install_allowed_obj single-object apply, its raise surface
    and the never-apply-cluster posture (./../lib/install_from_dir.sdd, which names this file
    as one of its two @install_allowed_obj callers); no other function of the module is used.
  /agent/templates/cronbackup.yaml.hbs (+ nested /agent/templates/backup.yaml.hbs Pod template)
    resolved through `context.template_dir` — the rendered manifest shape and the fixed
    `{appslug}-backup`/`-backups` object names are template-owned, not frozen here.
  vynil-core 0.7.7 engine API — the @import_run shim, @new_hbs + @register_partial_dir +
    @render_from of the `"HandleBars"` type (/common/src/handlebarshandler.sdd, helpers
    `json_to_str`/`selector_from_ctx`/`to_json` riding the same engine), @is_dir,
    @yaml_decode_multi, @type_of, @json_encode; the `switch type_of(e)` arms are plain rhai.
  /common/src/instancetenant.sdd + /common/src/instance_macros.sdd — the
    @set_status_schedule_backup_failed bridge on @TenantInstance and the
    `ScheduleBackup`/`ScheduleBackupFailed` condition it patches; /common/src/rhaihandler.sdd —
    the @Script constructor mounting all of the above.

References:
  ./install.rhai + ./install.sdd — the sole caller: the backup branch gate (status vitals +
    `context.namespace.use_backup` + readable `backup-settings` Secret in
    `context.instance.namespace`), the (instance, context) injection, the scalables-before-
    schedule comment dependency and the returned-context discard frozen there;
    /agent/src/tenant/install.sdd — the exit-1 error lane every raise here lands in, and the
    `args`/resolver-directory setup behind `context`.
  ./backup.sdd — the Pod-side lane the rendered cron dispatches (`agent <package.type> backup`
    on the tenant kind): it consumes, via @from_args, the envs @bctx::run baked into the Pod
    spec here.
  ./delete_backup.rhai — the cleanup lane matching these objects by fixed name/labels (spec
    pending); see Tasks for the name-mismatch finding at that seam (twin-shared).
  ../service/schedule_backup.sdd + ./../service/schedule_backup.rhai — the calibration twin:
    `diff` shows exactly one hunk (line 22, @install_obj → @install_allowed_obj); its
    RBAC-name `[?]` re-confirmed here as real.
  ./context.sdd + ./context_tenant.sdd — producers of the `tenant` mint, the `namespace`
    alias, the maintenance pair and the swallow-to-`""` failure mode of `tenant.namespaces`.
  ./../service/init_from.sdd — sibling stage with the opposite posture (dedup + wait + its own
    marker); this directory's ./init_from.rhai carries that shape for the tenant kind (no spec
    yet). Contrast only.
  /agent/tests/rhai_lifecycle.rs — service engines only; /agent/tests/rhai_backup_redis.rs —
    a tenant mock engine that never imports this module (Tasks).

Scenario: qualifying install re-applies the whole schedule
  Given the tenant install backup branch fired (vitals present, `use_backup` annotation true,
    `backup-settings` Secret readable), a context with `instance.appslug` `acme`, one scripts
    file under `${package_dir}/scripts` (`has_files` true), the minted tenant maintenance pair
    and no `pods/` dir
  When @run executes
  Then the four documents apply through @install_allowed_obj into
    `instance.metadata.namespace` in stream order with `metadata.namespace` rewritten:
    ConfigMap `acme-backups-scripts`, ServiceAccount `acme-backup`, RoleBinding `acme-backup`,
    CronJob `acme-backups` with `spec.schedule` `0 1 * * *`
  And @run returns `context`, which the caller discards, and no `status` write happens
  No mapped test.

Scenario: an own namespace routes, an out-of-list one remaps
  Given a `pods/cronbackup.yaml.hbs` shadow whose documents carry
    `.metadata: {namespace: ...}` — `team-a` (in `tenant.namespaces`) and `other-ns` (absent)
  When @run applies them
  Then the `team-a` document applies in `team-a` keeping its namespace, the `other-ns`
    document applies silently in `instance.metadata.namespace` rewritten, and a cluster-scoped
    kind in the same stream is warn-`Ignoring`ed with nothing applied (the service twin would
    have applied it under `allow_cluster` `true`)
  No mapped test.

Scenario: any raise becomes the marker and aborts the install
  Given @bctx::run dies on absent `status.vitals`, OR a document's kind is unknown to
    discovery, OR the CronJob apply hits a Forbidden
  When the raise enters the @catch
  Then the reason string is the error's own text (or its @json_encode) and
    @set_status_schedule_backup_failed patches `ScheduleBackup`/`Warning`
    `ScheduleBackupFailed` on the TenantInstance
  And the bare `throw;` crosses @import_run (not a skip marker), so no `install_post`, no
    `Ready`-close and exit 1; documents applied before a mid-loop failure stay applied
  No mapped test.

Scenario: package pods partial shadows the shipped one
  Given `${package_dir}/pods/cronbackup.yaml.hbs` ships a rewritten schedule section
  When @run registers partial directories
  Then the second registration wins, the package text renders instead of
    /agent/templates/cronbackup.yaml.hbs, and the loop applies whatever it yields — same
    posture for `pods/backup.yaml.hbs` replacing the Pod template
  No mapped test.

Scenario: hooks merge or skip without touching the schedule
  Given no hook module exists anywhere on the tenant resolver path
  When @run executes
  Then all three names debug-log their skip, no @type_of guard ever merges, and the lane runs
    on the untouched `context`
  But given a package `schedule_backup_prepare.rhai` whose run returns a map, a `pods`-style
    context tweak reaches the render
  And given that hook instead raises, the marker path writes `ScheduleBackupFailed` and
    re-throws
  No mapped test.

Example:
  Marker posture: thrown string `boom` → reason `boom`; thrown map → reason `{"k":"v"}` json
    text
  Schedule text: minted `maintenance_start_minut: "0"` + `..._hour: "1"` (./context.sdd) →
    CronJob `schedule: 0 1 * * *` (composed in @bctx::run, not here)

Tasks:
  [ ] Map the five Scenarios to tests: no test names this module —
      /agent/tests/rhai_lifecycle.rs is service-only and /agent/tests/rhai_backup_redis.rs
      builds a tenant mock engine but imports only the backup-prepare/restore modules; add the
      install/schedule harness (vitals-bearing mock tenant, template-dir fixtures, mock
      applies) before the TTD green gate (same harness task as ./context.sdd and
      ./install.sdd).
  [?] RBAC name seam — RE-CONFIRMED for the tenant lane (shared template, same names): this
      lane creates ServiceAccount AND RoleBinding `{appslug}-backup` (singular), while
      ./delete_backup.rhai deletes a RoleBinding named `{appslug}-backups` (plural,
      NotFound-swallowed) and never sweeps the ServiceAccount — disabling tenant backup leaves
      both behind. One decision for the twin pair: fix the shared template names or both
      delete lanes (mirrors ../service/schedule_backup.sdd).
  [?] Allow-list degeneracy: `context.tenant.namespaces` reaches @install_allowed_obj raw, and
      ./context.sdd shows it can land as the string `""` when the namespaces getter swallows a
      cluster failure — pin with a test what the allow check does with a non-array @allowed
      before deciding whether this file (or @install_allowed_obj) must guard the type.
  [?] Hook raises outside the @try (`schedule_backup_pre`, `schedule_backup_post`) abort with
      no marker — pre means "nothing scheduled, nothing recorded", post means "schedule
      applied, unrecorded, install failed". Decide whether the hooks join the guarded body
      (twin-shared: ../service/schedule_backup.sdd and ./../service/init_from.sdd carry the
      same open point).
  [?] The hook-merged context (and the final `context`) reaches no consumer: ./install.sdd
      discards this lane's return whole — confirm the hooks are intended as side-effect-only
      slots (twin-shared with ../service/schedule_backup.sdd).
  [?] `ScheduleBackup` has no success twin on @TenantInstance either: a correctly scheduled
      tenant never carries a positive condition, and reruns cannot distinguish "applied by a
      prior install" from an operator-edit of the same-named objects. Confirm the no-inventory
      posture is intended or add a success stamp (twin-shared).

Done when:
  Every Scenario carries a passing mock-engine test or a recorded decision, the render→apply
    order, the tenant allow-list routing, the `false` @bctx::run posture and the single marker
    call still match the source, the single-line twin diff against
    ./../service/schedule_backup.rhai still holds, and `specdd lint` is green on this file.
